  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...\nEarly this morning, a cold front has pushed across most of \ncentral Illinois and currently lingers near the I-70 corridor. \nTo the northwest, high pressure is centered over the Dakotas \nwith ridging extending southeast into portions of central \nIllinois. A sharp temperature gradient will be in place across \nthe region today across the stalled frontal zone. Highs \nnorthwest of the I-55 corridor are projected to top out in the \n70s, whereas locations south of I-70 will warm to around the 90\ndegree mark. \n\nUnderneath a strong mid-level (700-800 mb) warm nose, surface- \nbased instability will be virtually non-existent this afternoon.\nHowever, modest elevated instability (700-1000 J/kg) rooted \nabove the cap, combined with subtle isentropic ascent along the \n310-315K surfaces, may allow ongoing elevated showers and weak \nconvection over Iowa and Missouri early this morning to track \ninto central Illinois during the afternoon hours. Forcing \nremains fairly nebulous, and NBM mean 24-hour QPF has trended \nconsiderably downward over the past couple runs, showing around \na tenth of an inch of rain west of I-55 with lesser amounts \nfurther east. HREF LPMM guidance supports localized amounts \naround 0.10 inches. \n\nPrecipitation chances will diminish overnight as forcing shifts\nback west toward the mid-Missouri Valley on the nose of a low-\nlevel jet. Convection is expected to remain west of the local \narea through daybreak. Low temperatures tonight will fall into \nthe mid-50s to lower 60s under light north to northeast winds \nand lingering cloud cover.\n\nMonday through Wednesday...\nAn active and unsettled pattern is anticipated through the \nfirst half of the upcoming work week. A closed upper-level low \nover the Pacific Northwest will trek across the Canadian Rockies\nlate tonight into Monday. In response, a deep surface low will \nmature and track from the Canadian Prairies toward Hudson Bay \nMonday into Tuesday, dragging a cold front into the Midwest. \n\nWidespread showers and thunderstorms will arrive along the \ntrailing cold front Tuesday into Wednesday. Severe weather \npotential warrants close monitoring: the upper-level jet dipping\nacross the Upper Midwest will enhance deep-layer bulk shear \n(30-40 kt) over central Illinois. GFS guidance advertises \n1000-1500 J/kg MLCAPE near the stalling frontal boundary, \nwhich when combined with the favorable shear supports organized \nconvective threats. CSU machine learning probabilities highlight\na 5-15% risk for severe weather across central Illinois both \ndays and SPC has introduced a marginal risk for much of the area\nalong and north of I-70.\n\nIn addition to severe weather, heavy rainfall will be a primary\nconcern as multiple rounds of convection track eastward along \nthe slow-moving west-to-east oriented front. Through Wednesday \nnight, NBM mean 48-hour QPF indicates 1.00 inch of rainfall \nnorth of a Beardstown to Bloomington line, with 2.00-inch QPF \ncontours clipping far northwestern sections near Galesburg.\n\nThursday and Friday...\nUnsettled conditions look to persist into late next week as the\nbaroclinic zone remains draped across the region, serving as a \nfocus for daily precipitation chances. Model guidance exhibits \nnotable variance regarding frontal placement. The GFS builds a \nstronger surface high behind the front, shunting the boundary \nsouth of the Ohio Valley by late Thursday and bringing cooler, \ndrier conditions. Conversely, the ECMWF features a weaker, more \nprogressive ridge, keeping the frontal boundary stalled across \ncentral and northern Illinois with continued convective chances \nand a persistent thermal gradient. Given these discrepancies, \nbroad-brushed chance POPs remain in the forecast with low \nconfidence in temperatures which will be highly dependent on the\neventual frontal placement.",
